@@ -2781,3 +2781,52 @@ standing approval above. Applied as follows.
   protected-write authority (#188).
 - Live, the read failed on every maintenance pass from 16:43 UTC, so no closure,
   retry, delivery or follow-up pass ran for any target.
+
+## 2026-10-24: event-scoped CI failures are CI configuration, not the candidate's (#177)
+
+- CI attribution compares like for like by workflow event where both sides carry
+  one. A failed head check is judged by the base run of the same name and event
+  when the base has one. A head failure or timeout whose only green base
+  evidence ran under a different event (`push` on the base, `pull_request` or
+  `workflow_dispatch` on the head) is the `configuration` disposition
+  (`src/repair/ci-rejection.ts`). On 2026-10-20 every ai.ubq.fi repair PR failed
+  `rust-native`, `rust-wasm` and `validate` this way: the workflow scoped its
+  jobs by event and aborted on the head with "No Rust work selected" before any
+  test ran. Each CI fix then edited the protected workflow, was refused and
+  spent a charged retry.
+- A `configuration` disposition charges no CI fix or correction and starts no
+  model. The pre-review gate, the CI fix admission re-derivation and the
+  reviewed head's delivery gate park the record with an `other` blocker. Its
+  message is `CI_CONFIGURATION_PREFIX` followed by
+  `<check>(<conclusion> on <head event>; base success only on <base event>)`
+  entries. The detail names at most eight trusted check names and webhook event
+  names and says owner action is required on the target's CI workflow. No
+  launcher rule re-admits it. A head already reviewed clean on its base at the
+  pre-review gate is never parked there (the existing missing-acceptance rule).
+  It waits in the 30-minute backoff instead, as for a red base.
+- Precedence: a like-for-like candidate failure, then configuration, then a
+  running base check, then a red base, then unattributed. A red base run of a
+  different event is still a red base.
+- Events come from `readChecks(head, names, { events: true })`. The client joins
+  each check run's `check_suite.id` with the commit's `/actions/runs?head_sha=`
+  listing (`event`, `check_suite_id`). Only attribution reads ask for events:
+  the base read of the failed names, and a head re-read of the failed names only
+  when the attribution would charge a fix. The join is best effort. An
+  unreadable listing (no Actions read permission, rate limit, malformed page),
+  an ambiguous suite, a run of another commit or a non-webhook event name proves
+  nothing. Every event stays absent and attribution is exactly as before, so the
+  rule is inert for a credential without Actions read access. Events are in
+  memory only, never persisted.
+- Known consequence: a target whose base commits run CI only on `push` and whose
+  candidate heads run it only on `pull_request` has no like-for-like base
+  evidence. Every red candidate head there parks as configuration, and the
+  charged CI fix route does not fire. Sentinel's own `ci.yml` is that shape for
+  `sentinel/repair/*` branches (`push` only on `master`, `development` and
+  `codex/**`). Whether that is wanted, or the rule should also need a
+  configuration-abort log signature, is an owner decision.
+- Not implemented: the log-signature rule (no-work-selected, scope mismatch,
+  setup failure before the test command). The only job-log read is the matrix
+  cell's start-time CI evidence. That read is advisory and never changes
+  correction policy, and the attribution path has none. The deduplicated owner
+  follow-up issue is also not implemented. The only deduplicated target-issue
+  writer is the launcher's review follow-up pass, keyed to merged pull requests.
